ms\nSea level\nUK Tide Gauge Network\nHistorical UK tide gauge data\nInternational sea level\nHistorical BPR data\nArgo\nWhat is Argo?\nAccessing Argo Data\nBODC Argo DAC Role\nBODC Argo Data Management\nGEBCO gridded bathymetry data\nHigh volume data\nPublished data library\nContact us\nContact details\nStaff list\nYour comments\nWhat is BODC?\nOur history\nOur national role\nOur international role\nFreedom of Information\nBODC 5Oth Anniversary\nVacancies\nHome\nSearch the data\nSubmit your data\nResources\nAbout\nBack to top\nSearch the data\nBODC Cruise Metadata Report for RRS James Clark Ross (\n74JC\n) cruise JR15001 (AMT25, JR864)\nIntroduction\nThis report collates information from the BODC\ncruise inventory\nand\noceanographic\ndatabases. It presents\nCruise Inventory Information\nA summary of BODC data holdings for the cruise from the\nNational Oceanographic Database\nProjects Database\nReferences\n- including cruise tracks and cruise reports\nCruise Inventory Information\nLong name\nRRS James Clark Ross\n(\n74JC\n)\ncruise JR15001 (AMT25, JR864)\nShort name\nJR15001 (AMT25, JR864)\nObjectives and Narrative\nAMT25: The overall aim of the Atlantic Meridional Transect programme is: to quantify key biogeochemical and ecosystem processes and their inherent variability over extended time and spatial scales in the Atlantic Ocean.  This is achieved by executing an annually repeated meridional transect through contrasting oceanic provinces, ranging from oligotrophic deserts, to highly productive shelf seas.\nSpecific objectives:\n- To quantify the nature and causes of ecological and biogeochemical variability in planktonic ecosystems;\n- To quantify the effects of this variability on nutrient cycling, on biogenic export and on air-sea exchange of climate active gases;\n- To construct multi-decadal, multidisciplinary ocean time-series which are integrated within a wider \"Pole-to-pole\" observatory concept;\n- To provide essential sea-truth validation for current and next generation satellite missions;\n- To provide essential data for global ecosystem model development and validation;\n- To provide a valuable, highly sought after and unique training arena for the next generation of UK and international oceanographers.\nJR864: The oceanographic research ship RRS James Clark Ross attempted to physically and biologically characterise Ascension Island's shelf from 14th-18th October 2015. Multibeam sonar swath was used to generate the first open-access, high resolution mapping of Ascension Island's seabed between 100-1100m depth. These data was used to determine seabed profile and rugosity characteristics. Seabed substratum was investigated using a benthic camera lander (SUCS) and water column physico-chemistry was gained using CTD. The SUCS provided 500 photographs and video clips of benthos from 21 site/depth combinations, which showed a wide disparity in richness and density of biodiversity. These were ground-truthed by targeted Agassiz trawl samples to collect vouchers specimens for morphological and genetic identification. Specimens collected were photographed, fixed and then preserved in 96% ethanol for later genetic analyses. Preliminary analysis of the biodiversity captured was undertaken in the Conservation Department building, Georgetown, Ascension Island and indicated new records for the Island including first records of brachipods.\nShip\nRRS James Clark Ross\nDeparture Port\nImmingham, United Kingdom\nDeparture Date\n2015-09-15\nArrival Port\nStanley, Falkland Islands (Malvinas)\nArrival Date\n2015-11-04\nPrincipal Scientist(s)\nDavid Barnes (British Antarctic Survey), Jonathan Sharples (University of Liverpool Department of Earth, Ocean and Ecological Sciences), Tim Smyth (Plymouth Marine Laboratory)\nResponsible Organisation\nBritish Antarctic Survey\nAssociated Project(s)\nIHB Sea Areas Visited\nNorth Atlantic Ocean, South Atlantic Ocean\nMarsden Squares Visited\n111/145/146/301/302/338/375/4/40/412/449/76\nWork Area Description\nAscension Island\nUnable to lookup cruise bounds at this time\nWork Area Bounding Polygon\nSouthern Limit\nunknown\nNorthern Limit\nunknown\nWestern Limit\nunknown\nEastern Limit\nunknown\nCruise Inventory Datasets\nPhysical oceanography\nCTD stations\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n76\nDescription:  CTD profiles (salinity, temperature, oxygen concentration, fluorescence, transmittance, down-welling PAR, backscatter).\nOptics (eg underwater light levels)\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n46\nDescription:  Continuous underway measurements of chlorophyll fluorescence, total backscattering, acidified backscattering, acid-labile backscattering, temperature, salinity, absorbtion and attenuation from the ship's non-toxic seawater supply\nOptics (eg underwater light levels)\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n27\nDescription:  Optical measurements (beam attenuation, backscattering and absorption coefficients)\nOptics (eg underwater light levels)\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n42\nDescription:  Optical measurements of the ship's seawater supply (beam attenuation, backscattering and absorption coefficients)\nOptics (eg underwater light levels)\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n46\nDescription:  Continuous measurements of above-water radiometery using a hyperspectral radiometer (Satlantic HyperSAS) from dawn to dusk. Including downwelling irradiance, sky radiance and water-leaving radiance.\nSurface measurements underway (T,S)\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n45\nDescription:  Continuous underway data from the ship's navigation, bathymetry, surface and meteorological instrumentation.\nSurface measurements underway (T,S)\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n46\nDescription:  Continuous underway measurements of chlorophyll fluorescence, total backscattering, acidified backscattering, acid-labile backscattering, temperature, salinity, absorbtion and attenuation from the ship's non-toxic seawater supply\nTransparency (eg transmissometer)\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n45\nDescription:  Continuous underway data from the ship's navigation, bathymetry, surface and meteorological instrumentation.\nChemical oceanography\nAlkalinity\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n142\nDescription:  Samples collected for carbonate chemistry (stored).\nCarbon dioxide\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n38\nDescription:  Carbon dioxide partial pressure in water and air\nIsotopes\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n25\nDescription:  4-12 discrete samples every 3-4 stations were collected for Isotopes (?15N and ?18O of nitrate). Samples will be analysed using the denitrifier method at the University of Liverpool after the cruise.\nNitrate\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n74\nDescription:  Discrete samples for macronutrients collected from each CTD cast (>12 samples per cast)\nNitrite\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n74\nDescription:  Discrete samples for macronutrients collected from each CTD cast (>12 samples per cast)\nOxygen\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n685\nDescription:  Discrete oxygen samples collected from each CTD cast for calibration of the CTD oxygen optode (> 6 samples per cast)\nPhosphate\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n74\nDescription:  Discrete samples for macronutrients collected from each CTD cast (>12 samples per cast)\nSilicate\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n74\nDescription:  Discrete samples for macronutrients collected from each CTD cast (>12 samples per cast)\nTrace elements\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n24\nDescription:  Go Flo Bottles: 30m sampling depth during noon CTD. Analysis: bacterioplankton activity (amino acid uptake) and enumeration (flow cytometry); ambient iron concentration\nBiology and fisheries\nDissolved organic matter (inc DOC)\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n34\nDescription:  Seawater filtered for DOC, 7-15 discrete samples per station down to 500 m. Samples to be analysed at the University of Liverpool after the cruise.\nDissolved organic matter (inc DOC)\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n52\nDescription:  Seawater filtered for determnation of coloured dissolved organic matter (CDOM) per station down to 500m. Ultrapath with 200cm pathlenght was used for analysing the samples. The data will be calibrated at the University of Newcastle\nDissolved organic matter (inc DOC)\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n52\nDescription:  Samples from the sea surface microlayer (SML) were collected using the Garrett screen and analysed for CDOM. The data will be calibrated at the University of Newcastle.\nDissolved organic matter (inc DOC)\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n52\nDescription:  Samples were also collcted for CDOM analyses from the non-toxic ship's underway system. The data will be calibrated at the University of Newcastle.\nOther biological/fisheries measurement\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n52\nDescription:  Seawater samples were collected for determnation of surfactants down to 500m. 797VA computerace voltammetry (HMDE) was used for analysing the samples. The data will be calibrated at the University of Newcastle\nOther biological/fisheries measurement\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n52\nDescription:  Samples from the sea surface microlayer (SML) were collected using the Garrett screen and analysed for surfactants. The data will be calibrated at the University of Newcastle.\nOther biological/fisheries measurement\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n52\nDescription:  Samples were also collelcted for surfactant determination from the non-toxic ship's underway system. The data will be calibrated at the University of Newcastle.\nParticulate organic matter (inc POC, PON)\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n37\nDescription:  Seawater filtered for POC, 5-16 discrete samples per station down to 500 m. Samples to be analysed on a Carlo Erbo elemental analyser at the University of Liverpool after the cruise.\nParticulate organic matter (inc POC, PON)\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n110\nDescription:  Discrete samples from the ship's non-toxic supply for POC, PIC, coccolith enumeration, and biogenic silica\nParticulate organic matter (inc POC, PON)\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n74\nDescription:  Discrete samples from varying depths (always including surface and DCM) for POC, PIC, coccolith enumeration, biogenic silica, and scanning electron microscopy\nPelagic bacteria/micro-organisms\nQuantity:\nnumber of stations =\n75\nDescription:  Filtered seawater samples from 1%, 55%, 97% light depths collected for later DNA/RNA analysis (stored until Jul 2016) 6 samples per station\nPelagic bacteria/micro-organisms\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n24\nDescription:  Go Flo Bottles: 30m sampling depth during noon CTD. Analysis: bacterioplankton activity (amino acid uptake) and enumeration (flow cytometry); ambient iron concentration\nPelagic bacteria/micro-organisms\nQuantity:\nnumber of experiments =\n7\nDescription:  CO incubation experiment samples collected for later DNA/RNA analysis (stored) 12 samples per experiment\nPelagic bacteria/micro-organisms\nQuantity:\nnumber of stations =\n75\nDescription:  Gluteraldehyde preserved samples from 1%, 55%, 97% light depths collected for later flowcytometry and microscopy (stored until Jul 2016) 6 samples per station\nPhytoplankton\nQuantity:\nnumber of stations =\n32\nDescription:  3-6 discrete samples per station for microscopy (>10 ?m). To be analysed at the university of Liverpool after the cruise.\nPhytoplankton\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n63\nDescription:  Quantification of pico- and nanophytoplankton abundance from all depths between the surface and 200m from  CTD casts by flow cytometry\nPhytoplankton\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n177\nDescription:  Surface water was fixed with lugol's iodine neutral solution and with formaldehyde neutral 20%. Samples will be analysed in the lab at FCUL (Lisbon).\nPhytoplankton pigs (eg chlorophyll, fluorescence)\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n36\nDescription:  Discrete samples were collected for size-fractionated chlorophyll (6-12 samples per cast).\nPhytoplankton pigs (eg chlorophyll, fluorescence)\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n109\nDescription:  Discrete chl-a samples collected from the ship's non-toxic supply for calibration of the Surfmet fluorometer.\nPhytoplankton pigs (eg chlorophyll, fluorescence)\nQuantity:\nnumber of profiles =\n74\nDescription:  Discrete chl-a samples collected from each CTD cast for calibration of the CTD fluorometer (9 samples per cast).\nPhytoplankton pigs (eg chlorophyll, fluorescence)\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n86\nDescription:  This dataset refers specifically to samples collected for High Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC) from the ships underway system by PI-D. Samples will be analysed by HPLC at Plymouth Marine Laboratory after the cruise.\nPhytoplankton pigs (eg chlorophyll, fluorescence)\nQuantity:\nnumber of samples =\n291\nDescription:  This dataset refers specifically to samples collected for High Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC) from the Niskin bottles attached to the CTD by PI-D Seawater samples were collected from the predawn and noon CTD casts. Samples will be analysed by HPLC at Plymouth Marine Laboratory after the cruise.\nPhytoplankton pigs (eg chlorophyll, fluorescence)\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n46\nDescription:  Continuous underway measurements of chlorophyll fluorescence, total backscattering, acidified backscattering, acid-labile backscattering, temperature, salinity, absorbtion and attenuation from the ship's non-toxic seawater supply\nMeteorology\nIncident radiation\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n43\nDescription:  Continuous underway measurements of total upwelling radiance, downwelling sky radiance, and total downwelling irradiance\nRoutine standard measurements\nQuantity:\nactivity duration in days =\n45\nDescription:  Continuous underway data from the ship's navigation, bathymetry, surface and meteorological instrumentation.\nGeology and geophysics